 We shall abbreviate $\otimes_{\gsk \Gamma^{\fin}[*]}$, $\otimes_{\gsk \mG^{\fin}[*]}$ both as ``$\otimes$'' below when no confusion can arise.
 
-\begin{prop}[{\cite[Corollary~10.3, Proposition~10.10(1)]{hrushovski:kazhdan:integration:vf}}]\label{red:D:iso}
-$\Psi$ and ${\mu}\Psi$ are both isomorphisms of graded semirings.
+\begin{prop}[Positive tensor presentation]\label{red:D:iso}
+Let $T_+$ and $T_{\mu,+}$ be the sources of $\Psi$ and ${\mu}\Psi$,
+respectively. All definitions below use the original ground $\mathbb S$.
+For a $\RES$ object with underlying set $X$, put
+\[
+ \Lambda_X=\{\delta\in\Gamma\cap\dcl^{\mathrm{eq}}(\mathbb S):
+         \exists\ \mathbb S\text{-definable }c:X\to\delta^\sharp\}.
+\]
+Let $\mathcal C$ be the semiring congruence on $T_+$ generated by
+\[
+ [\bm X]_r\otimes[U]_m\ \sim\
+ [\bm X]_r\otimes[U+\delta e_i]_m,
+ \qquad \bm X\in\RES[r],\ U\in\Gamma[m],\ \delta\in\Lambda_X,
+\]
+where $m\geq1$ and $i$ is a coordinate index. Let $\mathcal C_\mu$
+be the semiring congruence on $T_{\mu,+}$ generated by
+\[
+ [\bm X]_r\otimes[(U,\sigma)]_m\ \sim\
+ [\bm X]_r\otimes[(U+\delta e_i,\sigma_\delta)]_m,
+ \qquad \sigma_\delta(\eta)=\sigma(\eta-\delta e_i)-\delta,
+\]
+with $\bm X=(X,f,\omega)\in\mgRES[r]$ and the same admissibility
+condition $\delta\in\Lambda_X$.
+The homomorphisms $\Psi$ and ${\mu}\Psi$ are surjective, with kernel
+congruences $\mathcal C$ and $\mathcal C_\mu$, respectively. They induce
+isomorphisms of graded semirings
+\[
+ T_+/\mathcal C\ \simeq\ \gsk\RV[*],\qquad
+ T_{\mu,+}/\mathcal C_\mu\ \simeq\ \gsk\mgRV[*].
+\]
 \end{prop}
+\begin{proof}
+The finite product normal form over the original ground partitions
+every $\RV[k]$ object into pieces isomorphic to
+$X\times U^\sharp$, where $X$ has finite Gamma support and has a
+finite label of degree $k-m$, and $U\in\Gamma[m]$. For a measured
+object the combined weight on a RES factor has finite original-ground
+Gamma image. Partition by these values and use the shared finite
+measured Gamma factor in degree zero to transfer each constant weight
+to the Gamma factor. We may therefore normalize every RES combined
+weight to $0$ and retain the whole weight as a definable function
+$\lambda$ on its Gamma factor. This gives surjectivity of both positive
+tensor maps, including finite and degree-zero factors.
+
+For an admissible $\delta$, choose a witness $c:X\to\delta^\sharp$.
+Multiplication of the $i$th free RV coordinate by $c(x)$ gives an
+original-ground definable bijection between the two product lifts;
+division by $c(x)$ is its inverse. Its Gamma-Jacobian is $\delta$,
+so the prescribed target form $\sigma_\delta$ makes the map measured.
+Thus the displayed generating pairs lie in the respective kernels.
+The pair depends on $\delta$, not on the choice of witness.
+
+For the converse, work in one total degree $k$ and represent the two
+positive tensors by finite disjoint unions of product objects.
+Let $m$ be the largest Gamma arity on either side of a definable
+RV bijection $F$ between the product unions. In total degree $k$, all
+maximal-arity factors have RES degree $k-m$. We induct on $m$.
+
+At a full-rank Gamma type in a source product and a residue type in
+its RES factor, the angular normal form of the graph gives, on a
+product neighbourhood,
+\[
+ F(x,u)=\bigl(h(x),c(x)u^M\bigr),\qquad
+ M\in\operatorname{GL}_m(\mathbb Z),\qquad
+ \vrv(c(x))=\boldsymbol\delta\text{ constant}.
+\]
+Here the Gamma rank and the total degree force the target free arity
+and the RES degree to agree with those of the source. The residue
+output depends only on $x$. After fixing $x$, the generic full-fibre
+monomial calculation gives coefficients over $\mathbb S(x)$, which
+are retained as definable functions of $x$. Injectivity forces the
+square exponent matrix to be unimodular. Finite Gamma support makes
+the values of those coefficients constant after a residue partition,
+and their values are definable over the original ground. Whole-fibre
+surjectivity then forces $h$ to be injective; shrinking and taking its
+image makes it a RES bijection. Product completeness and compactness
+extend these formulas to definable product neighbourhoods. No
+original-ground RV representative of $\boldsymbol\delta$ is chosen.
+
+Compactness supplies finitely many such product neighbourhoods, with
+exceptions in finitely many proper original-ground integral-character
+hyperplanes. A branch with smaller target Gamma arity lies entirely
+over such hyperplanes. Apply the same construction to $F^{-1}$ and
+reverse those matches too. A common finite Boolean partition of the
+source RES and Gamma factors selects disjoint product cells. The
+two-sided cut property gives product images and retains every original
+patch image, including the inverse cover. On each matched cell, the
+integral monomial part is already a Gamma morphism and the elementary
+pairs handle the coefficient translations one coordinate at a time.
+
+Keep the matched target boxes whole and partition their complements by
+a finite product grid. These are positive tensor identities on both
+sides. Restricting $F$ to the actual two complements supplies the
+residual comparison directly. Every nonempty residual maximal-arity
+Gamma factor is covered by the finite exceptional hyperplanes.
+Partition by the first applicable hyperplane. A primitive integral
+equation and a unimodular coordinate change put one coordinate at a
+fixed original-ground value. Finite-Gamma balancing transfers that
+coordinate into RES, lowering Gamma arity by one and preserving total
+degree. The old lower-arity factors already satisfy the smaller bound.
+Apply induction to the residual tensors and add the matched congruences.
+For $m=0$, all Gamma factors are finite and are absorbed into RES, where
+the positive RES embedding into RV gives equality. Finally reassemble
+the finitely many total degrees. This proves the unmeasured kernel
+assertion without cancelling matched summands.
+
+For the measured kernel, use the preceding normalization on both
+sides. A matched rectangle then satisfies
+\[
+ \lambda(\gamma)=\nu(M\gamma+\boldsymbol\delta).
+\]
+The unimodular Gamma morphism and the elementary translated pairs
+respect these complete combined weights. Retain each parent's Gamma
+weight on every cell of the finite product grid. On the disjoint union
+of native charts, the forms glue to an original-ground definable form;
+the complement inherits this form, and the restricted map $F$ preserves
+it. The two residual positive sums therefore present actual measured RV
+objects. In the hyperplane reduction, give the fixed finite Gamma
+prefix combined weight $0$ and substitute its value into the remaining
+Gamma weight function. Balancing transfers its degree into RES while
+retaining the full remaining weight. At rank zero absorb the actual
+finite measured Gamma object with its entire form into measured RES.
+The same positive induction and finite-degree reassembly prove the
+measured kernel assertion.
+
+Every step uses actual bijections, finite partitions, and positive
+semiring operations. Arithmetic in the Gamma-valued forms changes
+object data; it does not subtract semiring classes. The quotient
+isomorphisms follow from surjectivity and the kernel-congruence
+equalities, without additive cancellation or groupification.
+\end{proof}
